I tried `async move` and then it complains about moving out of `&mut self`. What's the right pattern here? Do I need Arc<Mutex<Self>> everywhere?

The spawned task has to be `'static`, so it cannot hold `&mut self`. Take what the task needs out of `self` first and move owned values in:

```rust
pub async fn run(&mut self) -> Result<(), SyncError> {
    let pending = std::mem::take(&mut self.pending);
    let client = self.client.clone();
    let cfg = self.cfg;
    let handle = tokio::spawn(async move {
        for entry in pending {
            push(&client, cfg, entry).await?;
        }
        Ok::<(), SyncError>(())
    });
    handle.await??;
    Ok(())
}

async fn push(client: &reqwest::Client, cfg: &'static Config, e: Entry) -> Result<(), SyncError> {
    let url = format!("{}/entries", cfg.base_url);
    client.post(url).json(&e).send().await?.error_for_status()?;
    Ok(())
}
```

`reqwest::Client` is cheap to clone (it holds an `Arc` inside), `cfg` is already `&'static`, and `std::mem::take` leaves an empty `Vec` behind. You only need `Arc<tokio::sync::Mutex<...>>` if other tasks keep adding entries while this one runs; nothing in the code you showed does that. You will also need `impl From<tokio::task::JoinError> for SyncError` for the double `?`.

